          "why": "Forgot to shift the angles by adding the phase shift $\\pi/3$."
        },
        {
          "text": "$x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$",
          "why": "Missed the second quadrant solution for sine."
        },
        {
          "text": "$x = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}, \\frac{4\\pi}{3}$",
          "why": "Used $\\cos(x - \\pi/3) = 1/2$ instead of sine."
        }
      ],
      "solution_text": "$2\\sin\\left(x - \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = 1$\n$\\sin\\left(x - \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\frac{1}{2}$\nFor $x \\in [0, 2\\pi]$, $x - \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\right]$.\n$x - \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{5\\pi}{6}$\n$x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{7\\pi}{6}$"
    },
