      ],
      "solution_text": "$\\sqrt{2}\\cos\\left(2x + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\sqrt{2}$\n$\\cos\\left(2x + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 1$\nFor $x \\in [0, 2\\pi]$, $2x + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\in \\left[\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{17\\pi}{4}\\right]$.\n$2x + \\frac{\\pi}{4} = 2\\pi, 4\\pi$\n$2x = \\frac{7\\pi}{4}, \\frac{15\\pi}{4}$\n$x = \\frac{7\\pi}{8}, \\frac{15\\pi}{8}$"
    },
    {
      "id": "q7",
      "question_text": "An alternating current in amperes is modelled by $I(t) = 3\\sin(100\\pi t) + 4\\cos(100\\pi t)$. What is the peak current?",
      "structure": "solve-auxiliary-application-context",
      "mastery": false,
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "$5\\text{ A}$",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "$7\\text{ A}$",
          "why": "Summed the amplitudes $3 + 4 = 7$ directly."
        },
        {
          "text": "$25\\text{ A}$",
          "why": "Computed $R^2 = 3^2 + 4^2 = 25$ without taking the square root."
        },
        {
          "text": "$1\\text{ A}$",
          "why": "Subtracted the amplitudes $4 - 3 = 1$."
        }
      ],
      "solution_text": "$I(t) = R\\sin(100\\pi t + \\alpha)$\n$R = \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5$\nPeak current is $5\\text{ A}$."
    },
